  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The atomic number of neodymium is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn></math>.</p><p>Therefore, the electronic configuration of neodymium (Nd) can be determined by distributing its electrons according to the Aufbau principle, Pauli exclusion principle, and Hund's rule.</p><p>Electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Nd</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi><mo> </mo><mo>=</mo><mo> </mo><mn>60</mn><mo>)</mo></math> is;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Xe</mi><mo>]</mo><mo> </mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p>as the last electron of Neodymium enters the f subshell. so it is called a f-block element.</p><p>Hence option A is the answer.</p>"
